[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"DDoS Mitigation and Traffic Analysis"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks remain one of the most pervasive threats to modern web infrastructure. Effective mitigation relies on robust traffic analysis, rapid anomaly detection, and automated remediation strategies to maintain service availability."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Traffic Baseline and Anomaly Detection"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before implementing mitigation controls, security analysts must establish a normal traffic baseline. By monitoring network metrics such as packets per second (PPS), bandwidth utilization, and TCP connection states over time, systems can detect deviations that signal an ongoing volumetric or application-layer attack."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Analyzing NetFlow and sFlow Data"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"NetFlow and sFlow protocols provide visibility into network traffic flows without capturing full packet payloads. The following command demonstrates how to inspect incoming traffic statistics using a command-line tool like tcpdump to identify high-frequency SYN packets."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"sudo tcpdump -nnn -i eth0 'tcp[tcpflags] & (tcp-syn) != 0' -c 100"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Mitigation Strategies"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rate Limiting: Restricting the number of requests a single IP address can make within a specified timeframe to prevent resource exhaustion."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Anycast Routing: Distributing incoming traffic across multiple geographically dispersed scrubbing centers to absorb volumetric spikes."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SYN Cookies: Allowing the server to respond to TCP connection requests without allocating memory until the handshake is fully completed, mitigating SYN flood attacks."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing NGINX Rate Limiting"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"At the application layer, configuring a reverse proxy like NGINX helps mitigate HTTP flood attacks. The configuration snippet below defines a limit zone allowing 10 requests per second per IP address."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"http {
    limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=one:10m rate=10r/s;
    server {
        location /login {
            limit_req zone=one burst=5 nodelay;
            proxy_pass http://backend;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mitigating DDoS attacks requires a multi-layered defense architecture. Combining continuous traffic analysis using NetFlow, edge-filtering via Anycast networks, and strict application-layer rate limiting ensures high resilience against evolving threat vectors."}]
